at is an Enum?

An enum is a special data type that represents a fixed set of constants.

Instead of writing:

String day = "Monday";

someone might accidentally write:

day = "Mondy";

The compiler won't catch the typo.

Instead, use an enum:

enum Day {
    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY,
    WEDNESDAY
}

Now:

Day day = Day.MONDAY;

Only the defined values are allowed.

Why do we use Enums?

Suppose you're creating an order system.

Without enum:

String status = "Pending";
status = "Pendding";   // Typo
status = "Finished";   // Invalid

With enum:

enum OrderStatus {
    PENDING,
    SHIPPED,
    DELIVERED,
    CANCELLED
}

Now:

OrderStatus status = OrderStatus.PENDING;

Only valid values can be assigned.

Syntax
enum Day {

    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY,
    WEDNESDAY,
    THURSDAY,
    FRIDAY,
    SATURDAY,
    SUNDAY

}

Use it like this:

Day today = Day.FRIDAY;
What actually happens behind the scenes?

When you write:

enum Day {
    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY
}

The compiler roughly creates something like:

final class Day extends Enum<Day> {

    public static final Day MONDAY = new Day();

    public static final Day TUESDAY = new Day();

    private Day() {}
}

Notice:

It's a class.
Constructor is private.
Objects are created automatically.
You cannot create new enum objects.

This is illegal:

new Day();    // Error
Enum Objects

Every constant is actually an object.

Day.MONDAY

is an object.

Day.TUESDAY

is another object.

Enum Methods

Java provides several useful methods.

values()

Returns all constants.

for (Day d : Day.values()) {
    System.out.println(d);
}

Output:

MONDAY
TUESDAY
WEDNESDAY
...
valueOf()

Convert String to enum.

Day day = Day.valueOf("MONDAY");

System.out.println(day);

Output:

MONDAY

Wrong value:

Day.valueOf("Monday");

Throws:

IllegalArgumentException

because enums are case-sensitive.

ordinal()

Returns the position.

System.out.println(Day.MONDAY.ordinal());

Output

0
MONDAY -> 0
TUESDAY -> 1
WEDNESDAY -> 2

Don't store ordinal() values in a database, because changing the enum order changes the ordinals.

name()

Returns the constant name.

System.out.println(Day.FRIDAY.name());

Output

FRIDAY
Enum in switch

One of the most common uses.

Day day = Day.MONDAY;

switch(day){

case MONDAY:
    System.out.println("Start");
    break;

case FRIDAY:
    System.out.println("Weekend coming");
    break;

default:
    System.out.println("Normal Day");

}
Can Enum Have Variables?

Yes.

enum Laptop {

    HP(50000),
    DELL(60000),
    ASUS(70000);

    private int price;

    Laptop(int price){
        this.price = price;
    }

    public int getPrice(){
        return price;
    }

}

Use:

System.out.println(Laptop.HP.getPrice());

Output

50000
Enum Constructors

Constructors are allowed.

enum Car {

    BMW(80),
    AUDI(90);

    int speed;

    Car(int speed){
        this.speed = speed;
    }

}

Constructor is automatically called when constants are created.

Can Enum Have Methods?

Absolutely.

enum Operation {

    ADD,
    SUBTRACT;

    public void display(){

        System.out.println("Calculator");

    }

}

Use:

Operation.ADD.display();
Enum with Multiple Variables
enum EmployeeRole {

    ADMIN(1,"Full Access"),
    USER(2,"Limited Access"),
    GUEST(3,"Read Only");

    private int id;
    private String description;

    EmployeeRole(int id,String description){

        this.id=id;
        this.description=description;

    }

    public int getId(){
        return id;
    }

    public String getDescription(){
        return description;
    }

}
Can Enum Implement Interface?

Yes.

interface Printable{

    void print();

}

enum Color implements Printable{

    RED,
    GREEN;

    public void print(){

        System.out.println("Printing");

    }

}
Can Enum Extend Another Class?

No.

class Animal{}

enum Dog extends Animal{}

Not allowed.

Reason:

Enums already extend:

Enum<E>

Java doesn't support multiple inheritance of classes.

Can Enum Be Inherited?

No.

enum A{}

enum B extends A{}

Not allowed.

Enums are implicitly final.

Abstract Methods in Enum

Yes.

enum Operation {

    ADD{
        public int calculate(int a,int b){
            return a+b;
        }
    },

    MULTIPLY{
        public int calculate(int a,int b){
            return a*b;
        }
    };

    public abstract int calculate(int a,int b);

}

Use:

System.out.println(Operation.ADD.calculate(10,20));

Output

30

Each enum constant provides its own implementation.

Enum in Collections
Set<Day> holidays = EnumSet.of(
        Day.SATURDAY,
        Day.SUNDAY
);

EnumSet is extremely fast because it's optimized specifically for enums.

Similarly:

Map<Day,String> map = new EnumMap<>(Day.class);
Enums in Spring Boot

Suppose you have:

enum Role {

    ADMIN,
    USER

}

Entity:

@Entity
class User{

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    private Role role;

}

Database:

ADMIN

Instead of

0

Using EnumType.STRING is usually recommended because it's more readable and doesn't break if you reorder enum constants.

Real-world Uses

Enums are commonly used for:

User roles (ADMIN, USER)
Order status (PENDING, SHIPPED, DELIVERED)
Payment status (SUCCESS, FAILED, REFUNDED)
Days of the week
Months
HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
Traffic lights
Game states
Directions (NORTH, SOUTH, EAST, WEST)
Interview Questions
1. Is an enum a class?

Yes. It is a special type of class that extends java.lang.Enum.

2. Can an enum have constructors?

Yes, but they are implicitly private. You cannot create enum instances yourself.

3. Can an enum have methods?

Yes. Both instance methods and abstract methods are supported.

4. Can an enum implement interfaces?

Yes.

5. Can an enum extend another class?

No. It already extends java.lang.Enum.

6. Are enum constants objects?

Yes. Every constant is a singleton object created by the JVM.

7. Why use EnumType.STRING in JPA?

Because storing names like ADMIN is stable and readable, whereas storing ordinals like 0 can cause bugs if you reorder the enum constants.

