Excellent question. This is the point where many Java learners get confused. Let's break it down step by step.

When you write:

enum Day {
    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY
}

you only see 2 lines, but the Java compiler generates something similar (not exactly identical) to this:

final class Day extends Enum<Day> {

    public static final Day MONDAY = new Day();

    public static final Day TUESDAY = new Day();

    private Day() {}
}

Let's understand every line.

1. final class Day
final class Day

This means the enum is actually a class.

It is final because Java does not allow inheritance from an enum.

For example:

enum Day {}

class MyDay extends Day {}   // ❌ Error
2. extends Enum<Day>
extends Enum<Day>

Every enum automatically extends Java's built-in class:

java.lang.Enum

That is why every enum already has methods like

name()
ordinal()
compareTo()

You never wrote them.

Example:

Day.MONDAY.name();

works because Day extends Enum.

3. public static final Day MONDAY

This is the most important line.

public static final Day MONDAY = new Day();

This means

MONDAY is actually an object.

Think about a normal class.

class Student {

}

Create objects:

Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();

Exactly the same thing happens internally.

Java creates

Day MONDAY = new Day();

and

Day TUESDAY = new Day();

So when you write

Day.MONDAY

you are accessing an object.

Visualize it
Heap Memory

+------------------+
| Day Object       |
| (MONDAY)         |
+------------------+

+------------------+
| Day Object       |
| (TUESDAY)        |
+------------------+

There are only these two objects.

4. Why static?
public static final Day MONDAY

Static means

"There is only one copy."

You don't need an object to access it.

You simply write

Day.MONDAY

instead of

new Day().MONDAY
5. Why final?
public static final Day MONDAY

Final means

You cannot change it.

Imagine

Day.MONDAY = Day.TUESDAY;

Not allowed.

The constant always refers to the same object.

6. Why is the constructor private?
private Day(){}

Suppose it were public.

Then you could do

Day d = new Day();

Now there would be a third object.

Java doesn't want that.

Enums should only have the predefined constants.

So Java hides the constructor.

Only the JVM creates

MONDAY
TUESDAY

Nobody else can.

Think of it like this

Suppose you wrote

class Day {

}

You could create

new Day();
new Day();
new Day();
new Day();

Unlimited objects.

But with an enum

enum Day {
    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY
}

Java says

"Only these objects exist."

MONDAY ✅

TUESDAY ✅

WEDNESDAY ❌

new Day() ❌
Why does Day.MONDAY work?

Because internally Java has

public static final Day MONDAY = new Day();

So

Day.MONDAY

is simply accessing that static object.

Just like

class Math {

    public static final double PI = 3.14;

}

You write

Math.PI

Similarly,

Day.MONDAY

accesses the static enum object.

A more realistic picture

Internally, Java's generated code is closer to this:

final class Day extends Enum<Day> {

    public static final Day MONDAY = new Day("MONDAY", 0);
    public static final Day TUESDAY = new Day("TUESDAY", 1);

    private Day(String name, int ordinal) {
        super(name, ordinal);
    }

    public static Day[] values() {
        return new Day[]{MONDAY, TUESDAY};
    }

    public static Day valueOf(String name) {
        // Returns the matching enum constant
    }
}

Notice that each constant has:

A name ("MONDAY")
An ordinal (0)

That's why these work:

System.out.println(Day.MONDAY.name());     // MONDAY
System.out.println(Day.MONDAY.ordinal());  // 0
The most important thing to remember

An enum like:

enum Day {
    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY
}

is not just a list of words. It behaves like a class that already contains a fixed set of pre-created objects:

Day.MONDAY  // Object #1
Day.TUESDAY // Object #2

There are only these objects, because the constructor is private and the fields are public static final. This design makes enums perfect for representing a fixed set of values.

